`]}),e.jsx(u,{}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Training records ranked merges",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) is a widely used family of methods for learning a subword vocabulary ",e.jsx(n,{id:"sennrich2016",children:"Sennrich et al., 2016"}),". Sennrich-style BPE starts from characters, byte-level BPE (the Bytes tab) from bytes. Both learn an ordered list of merges."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Training starts from a corpus split into base symbols. It counts every pair of adjacent symbols, picks the most frequent pair, merges every occurrence of that pair into one new symbol, and records the merge with its rank, its position in the list, rank 1 first. Then it counts again on the merged corpus and repeats. When several pairs share the highest count, an explicit tie rule picks one, so training gives the same result every time. Training stops at a declared target, such as a number of merges, a vocabulary size, or a minimum pair frequency."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'The widget trains BPE on the phrase "the lower the better", with characters as base symbols, which it lists as its initial alphabet, and ␣ for the space. When counts tie, it takes the pair whose first occurrence comes earliest in the current corpus. It stops when no adjacent pair occurs more than once.'}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget learns four merges, each from a pair that occurs twice: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t + h → th"}),", then ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"th + e → the"}),", then ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"the + ␣ → the␣"}),", and finally ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"e + r → er"}),". After the fourth merge every remaining pair occurs only once, and the stop rule ends training. Without the stop rule, the tie rule would pick among pairs that each occur once, and that choice would mean nothing."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Once training stops, the widget encodes a new string, "the lower", by applying the four frozen ranks in order. Its nine symbols become five tokens, ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[the␣, l, o, w, er]"}),". An implementation therefore stores an ordered merge table. A set of frequent strings would not say which merge to apply first."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The simulation is a toy. A production tokenizer uses a different base, splits the text before merging, aims at a target vocabulary size, and has its own stopping rule."})]})]}),e.jsx(p,{caption:"After the fourth merge no pair occurs twice, so training stops. The four frozen ranks then encode “the lower” as five tokens: the␣, l, o, w, er.",children:e.jsx(u,{initialStep:4})}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"BPE learns an ordered list of merges",accent:1,number:"3",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(g,{name:"BPE",children:"Byte Pair Encoding"})," (BPE) ",e.jsx(n,{id:"sennrich2016",children:"Sennrich et al., 2016"})]}),`
