`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Coverage and reversibility are different questions",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Coverage asks whether every input has a representation. Reversibility asks whether decoding returns exactly the text that came in. The two can come apart, because Unicode can write the same visible character in more than one way. Composed, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"é"})," is one code point. Decomposed, in NFD (Normalization Form D), it is two: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"e"})," followed by a combining acute accent."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A tokenizer with full byte coverage represents both byte sequences. If the pipeline normalizes its input to NFC first, though, an NFD input comes back as NFC after decoding. It looks the same and has different code points. Case conversion, whitespace cleanup and a policy that rejects some inputs break the round trip in the same way. Monad itself applies no normalization, so an NFD ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"é"})," is encoded as its three bytes and decodes back exactly. The sentences of the comparison widget in the Vocabulary tab, by contrast, were converted to NFC before they were tokenized."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"So when a round trip changes a text although every byte is covered, look first at the stages around the tokenizer. These policies also change what a tokenizer comparison measures, which is why each result needs them next to it."})]})]})]}),`
