`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"When text turns into control tokens",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Special tokens are matched in raw text. Monad encodes the string ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"end<|end_of_text|>next"})," as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"<|begin_of_text|> · end · <|end_of_text|> · n · ext"}),", so the literal characters become the real EOS with ID 2. A web page that happens to contain the string would insert a document boundary where there is none. A serving stack that encodes user text this way lets a user do the same to a deployed model. A pipeline that wants no boundaries from raw text has to remove or escape such strings, or encode raw text without matching special tokens. OpenAI's tiktoken, for example, by default raises an error when a text contains the string of a special token ",e.jsx(n,{id:"tiktoken-0.12.0",children:"OpenAI, 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A chat template formats a conversation as text with role markers, and many tokenizers add special tokens for the markers. Llama 3, for example, has ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"<|start_header_id|>"})," for a role header and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"<|eot_id|>"})," for the end of a turn ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama3-tokenizer-code",children:"Meta, 2024"}),". The course's template in Week 11 introduces no new special tokens, so the same EOS that separates documents in pre-training also ends a turn in a conversation."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"UTF-8 as a prefix code",optional:!0,accent:7,number:"22",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
