`]}),e.jsx(r,{title:"How a decoder finds each character",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A byte-level token can hold part of a character, so a decoder has to tell which bytes belong together. UTF-8 makes that possible. It is a prefix code, so no character's bytes begin another character's bytes ",e.jsx(n,{id:"rfc3629",children:"Yergeau, 2003"}),". The first byte of a character tells how many bytes follow, and every following byte starts with the bits ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"10"}),". So a decoder always knows where a character starts. The byte values ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"C0"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"C1"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"F5"})," to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"FF"})," never occur in valid UTF-8, and they are among the byte symbols that Monad's vocabulary lacks. The encoding is used by 99.1% of the websites whose encoding is known ",e.jsx(n,{id:"w3techs2026encoding",children:"W3Techs, 2026"}),"."]})})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Unused rows in the vocabulary matrix",optional:!0,accent:0,number:"23",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
