`]})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The answer, and the course's choice",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Raw bytes cover every input, but they cost one position per byte. At the slide's rate of about four bytes per token, byte input would need about four times as many positions for the same text, so a context window of fixed length would hold about a quarter as much. Byte-level BPE keeps the coverage of bytes, as long as all 256 byte symbols stay in the vocabulary, and spends vocabulary rows to buy shorter sequences. How many rows, which merges, and whether every byte symbol is kept are choices you measure on the target data."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Monad shows both sides. Its learned merges take the English sentence of the opening question from 72 bytes to 10 tokens, while the comparison widget's Japanese and Bengali sentences stay at one token per byte."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course reuses Monad with its 8,192 logical vocabulary entries, the IDs the tokenizer can produce ",e.jsx(n,{id:"monad-2025",children:"PleIAs, 2025"}),". Reusing a tokenizer lets the course spend its time on the model, training and optimization, while you can still measure what segmentation does. Another language, domain, context budget or deployment target may call for a different tokenizer."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Three takeaways on tokenization",accent:3,number:"25",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
