`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The chapter in three takeaways",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Every vocabulary row costs parameters and can buy shorter sequences, while more rows spread the same training text over more pieces. No size wins everywhere. Fertility, PCW, and tokens per byte and per code point describe the candidates on a named sample, and target tasks and runtime measurements decide whether to keep a tokenizer or to train a new one."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"BPE learns a ranked list of merges, and encoding replays it inside the chunks that pre-tokenization cuts. Underneath is the byte base, which covers every input only when all 256 byte symbols are in the vocabulary. Whether a text survives its round trip depends on the normalization and cleanup around the tokenizer."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In numbers, the chunk and merge rules decide where token boundaries fall relative to place value. That changes the structure a model has to learn for arithmetic, so test the formats a system serves."})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Reflection questions",accent:4,number:"26",items:"4",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"No submission. Bring short answers to the Week 2 tutorial."}),e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
